From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:41:14 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Restore the augmentation behaviour albumentations 1.4.1 had

Patch 0010 replaced albumentations with a vendored module, and 0032 pinned its
geometry against albumentations 1.4.1 and found it bit-exact. What it also found
were four behaviours that are not geometry and do not match, three of which a
stock `aug_default.yaml` training run reaches on every batch. None of them
raises; each one silently changes what the model trains on, which is exactly
what a "no behaviour change" port is supposed to rule out.

`GaussNoise` drew about 24% less noise than the reference. albumentations
sampled a *variance* uniformly and took its root, so `gaussian_noise: 12.75`
gave sigma = sqrt(U(0, 12.75**2)): mean 8.448, standard deviation 3.040. The
vendored transform took a `std_range` as a fraction of 255 and sampled it
uniformly -- 255 * U(0, 0.05): the same support, mean 6.408, standard deviation
3.668. The `std_range` name and the fraction-of-255 convention come from
albumentations 2.x, whose distribution is the uniform one; carrying the 2.x
spelling while owing the 1.4.1 distribution is what let the two drift apart, so
the parameter goes back to 1.4.1's `var_limit` and `mean` and `build_transforms`
back to the `var_limit=(0, noise**2), mean=0` call it made before 0010. Measured
after the change: mean 8.494, standard deviation 3.003, KS D = 0.0029 against
the reference sample (p = 0.36).

`MotionBlur` blurred systematically harder. albumentations drew the kernel size
from `range(3, 8, 2)`, so P(3) = P(5) = P(7) = 1/3 and the mean kernel was 4.99.
The vendored transform drew every integer in [3, 7] and rounded even sizes up,
which folds 4 into 5 and 6 into 7 and leaves 3 alone: 0.199 / 0.402 / 0.399,
mean 5.40, an 8% wider blur. Drawing from the sizes the limits actually allow
gives 0.336 / 0.333 / 0.332 and mean 4.99 again.

Bounding boxes were clipped once against the final canvas instead of after every
transform. albumentations ran its box filter after each transform in the
pipeline, clipping to the image that transform left behind and dropping the
boxes with nothing left; the vendored `Compose` clipped only at the end. The two
agree until a transform moves annotations back *into* the frame after an earlier
one pushed them out -- which is what padding does, and both `crop_sampling` in
`aug_default.yaml` and `auto_padding` in the hrnet and dekr configs put padding
directly after `affine`. A box pushed past the frame edge by a rotation or a
scaling therefore came back with its excursion intact, extending into the black
padding over pixels it never covered. Driving the stock config with pinned
affine parameters, 11 of 12 draws produced a different box, by up to 74 px on a
448 canvas; after the change, 0 of 12.

That last one is not confined to metadata. `data/dataset.py` computes each
instance's `area` as the width times the height of its augmented box, and
`models/target_generators/dekr_targets.py` weights the DEKR offset loss by
`1 / sqrt(area)` and resolves overlapping instances by comparing areas, so a box
that keeps its excursion changes the loss weights of every pixel it covers. The
torchvision detector regresses `labels["boxes"]` directly, though
`base_detector.yaml` has no padding after its affine and so is unaffected as
shipped, and `runners/train.py` rescales evaluation predictions with the same
boxes. The bottom-up heatmap and PAF losses do not read boxes at all.

Fourth, an `affine:` block naming only some of `rotation`, `scaling` and
`translation` raised `TypeError`, either at build time or on the first batch.
`config/data.py` types the block `dict | None` and validates it by calling
`build_transforms`, so a partial block is a configuration a user is invited to
write, and albumentations answered it with scale 1, rotation 0 and no
translation. `Affine` now defaults each unset parameter to its identity. An
entirely empty `affine: {}` stays the identity here rather than reproducing
albumentations' answer to it -- a random scale, translation, rotation *and*
shear -- which would mean inventing a shear the module has no other use for and
turning a block that asks for nothing into an augmentation.

Tests: the three strict xfails in `tests/test_augmentation_geometry.py` that
recorded these deviations are removed, since they now XPASS. The two remaining
ones -- the crop larger than the image, and `Compose(seed=...)` re-seeding on
every call -- are untouched. In their place the file gains the reference
distributions for both sampled parameters (kernel-size probabilities and mean;
sigma's mean, standard deviation and a KS test against `P(sigma <= s) = s**2 /
var_limit`), the `gaussian_noise` config wiring, four clipping cases measured on
albumentations 1.4.1 -- padding after a translation, a box dropped along with
its label before the padding, a rotation clipped to the rotated frame, and the
`crop_sampling` shape -- and the partial and empty affine blocks compared
against the pinned affine geometry the file already pins. Thirteen new source
mutations join the thirty-four already there, among them drawing sigma
uniformly, rounding even kernel sizes up, clipping only against the final
canvas, and defaulting a missing affine parameter to something other than the
identity. All forty-seven are killed; the suite is 58 passed, 2 xfailed.
